    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-qft-quartic-graph-power-counting",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Finite quartic graph and superficial scaling conventions",
        "deps": [
          "def-multigraph-and-digraph-degrees-and-connectivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-multigraph-and-digraph-degrees-and-connectivity": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alexander M. Polyakov's QFT I course material, reconstructed and expanded by QFT.org, lesson 39: One-Loop Divergences and Power Counting",
              "url": "https://qft.org/courses/qft-i/39-one-loop-divergences-and-power-counting/"
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, §18, equations (18.2)–(18.8)",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$ be the number of Euclidean momentum coordinates. Use the finite undirected multigraph convention of [[def-multigraph-and-digraph-degrees-and-connectivity]]: loops are allowed, distinct edges with the same endpoints are parallel, and connectedness here means that the underlying simple graph obtained by deleting loops and identifying each parallel class is connected.\n\nA **finite quartic graph** is data $\\Gamma=(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal E_{\\rm int},\\eta,\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext},(S_v)_{v\\in\\mathcal V},a)$ with the following properties. The vertex set $\\mathcal V$ is finite and nonempty, the internal-edge set $\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}$ and external-half-edge set $\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}$ are finite, and $\\eta:\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}\\times\\{0,1\\}\\to\\mathcal V$ assigns the two (unoriented, but separately labelled) ends of each internal edge to vertices. Its endpoint set is $\\{\\eta(e,0),\\eta(e,1)\\}$, a singleton for a loop; distinct elements of $\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}$ with the same unordered endpoints remain distinct parallel edges. The internal multigraph $(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal E_{\\rm int},\\eta)$ is connected in the sense just specified.\n\nFor every $v\\in\\mathcal V$, the incidence-slot set $S_v$ has exactly four elements. Set $\\mathcal S:=\\bigsqcup_{v\\in\\mathcal V}(\\{v\\}\\times S_v)$. The attachment map\n\n$$a:(\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}\\times\\{0,1\\})\\sqcup\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal S$$\n\nis a bijection, and for each internal end $(e,j)$ its slot has vertex coordinate $\\eta(e,j)$. Thus the two ends of a loop occupy distinct slots. Each external half-edge $h\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}$ is attached by $a(h)$ to exactly one vertex slot and has no second endpoint. In particular, each vertex has exactly four slots and every slot is used exactly once by an internal-edge end or an external half-edge. Write $V:=|\\mathcal V|$, $I:=|\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}|$, and $E:=|\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}|$.\n\nDefine the cycle rank and loop count by $L:=I-V+1$. For this mathematical power-counting convention, a single formal loop-momentum integration has assigned degree $+D$: for $\\ell\\in\\mathbb R^D$, Lebesgue measure $d^D\\ell$ acquires the factor $\\lambda^D$ under $\\ell\\mapsto\\lambda\\ell$ for $\\lambda>0$. The total loop contribution is defined to be $DL$, the cycle rank multiplied by this one-loop degree; no momentum routing or actual graph integral is part of this definition. Assign degree $-2$ to each internal scalar-propagator factor and degree $0$ to each quartic-vertex factor. For $q=(q_1,\\ldots,q_D)\\in\\mathbb R^D\\setminus\\{0\\}$ use the Euclidean norm $|q|^2:=\\sum_{j=1}^Dq_j^2$. For example, if $m\\ge0$, then $(|q|^2+m^2)^{-1}$ has assigned ultraviolet degree $-2$, since\n\n$$\\lim_{\\lambda\\to\\infty}\\lambda^2(|\\lambda q|^2+m^2)^{-1}=|q|^{-2}.$$\n\nThe **superficial degree** of $\\Gamma$ under this assignment is\n\n$$\\omega_D(\\Gamma):=DL-2I.$$\n\nBy convention, $\\omega_D(\\Gamma)=0$ is called a **logarithmic superficial degree**. This is finite graph bookkeeping under the stated assignment. It does not by itself establish convergence or divergence of an actual graph integral; proper subgraphs, numerator cancellations, exceptional infrared data, and distributional singularities require separate hypotheses and analysis."
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-qft-quartic-superficial-degree",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "Quartic graph superficial degree from exact counting",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-quartic-graph-power-counting"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-quartic-graph-power-counting": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Alexander M. Polyakov's QFT I course material, reconstructed and expanded by QFT.org, lesson 39: One-Loop Divergences and Power Counting",
              "url": "https://qft.org/courses/qft-i/39-one-loop-divergences-and-power-counting/"
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, §18, equations (18.2)–(18.8)",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$ and let $\\Gamma$ be a finite quartic graph in the sense of [[def-qft-quartic-graph-power-counting]]. With $V$ vertices, $I$ internal edges, $E$ external half-edges, and loop count $L$, the incidence and loop-count identities are $4V=2I+E$ and $L=I-V+1$. Its superficial degree under the stated Euclidean scalar power-counting convention is $\\omega_D(\\Gamma)=DL-2I=D+(D-4)V-\\frac{D-2}{2}E$. In $D=4$ this reduces to $\\omega_4(\\Gamma)=4-E$. A zero value is a logarithmic **superficial** degree by the convention in the definition; these identities do not assert convergence or divergence of an actual graph integral."
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-qft-scalar-engineering-dilation",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "Scalar engineering dimensions and tree-level dilation",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, §12, equations (12.5)–(12.11)",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §3 Interacting Fields, equations (3.202)–(3.204)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$, let $\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^D;\\mathbb R)$, let $r\\ge3$ be an integer, and let $g_r\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be a real scalar. The Euclidean gradient is $\\nabla_E\\phi:=(\\partial_1\\phi,\\ldots,\\partial_D\\phi)$ and its squared norm is $|\\nabla_E\\phi|^2:=\\sum_{j=1}^D(\\partial_j\\phi)^2$. With Lebesgue measure $d^Dx$ on $\\mathbb R^D$, define the real-valued functionals\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{kin}}(\\phi):=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^D}|\\nabla_E\\phi(x)|^2\\,d^Dx,\\qquad S_r(\\phi):=g_r\\int_{\\mathbb R^D}\\phi(x)^r\\,d^Dx.$$\n\nThey are finite because $\\phi$ and its derivatives have compact support. In the natural-unit mass-dimension convention stated below, the engineering dimensions are $[\\phi]=(D-2)/2$ and $[g_r]=D-r(D-2)/2$. Under the dilation $\\phi_b(x)=b^{-(D-2)/2}\\phi(x/b)$ for $b>0$, $S_{\\mathrm{kin}}(\\phi_b)=S_{\\mathrm{kin}}(\\phi)$ and $S_r(\\phi_b)=b^{D-r(D-2)/2}S_r(\\phi)$. Call the monomial relevant, marginal, or irrelevant at tree level according as this dilation exponent is positive, zero, or negative. Thus a quartic monomial has exponent $4-D$: it is relevant for $D<4$, marginal by engineering dimension for $D=4$, and irrelevant for $D>4$. These labels concern only this classical engineering-dimension convention; no quantum correction, fixed point, or renormalization-group flow is asserted."
      }
    ]
  },
